[38;5;231m⏺[39m Approved on both. One wrinkle the test decision surfaces, then I need your real URLs.

  [1mThe testing constraint[0m

  [38;5;153msettings.js[39m reads [38;5;153mwindow.location.hostname[39m at module load. Import it in Node and that line throws before any test
  runs. Two ways out:

  - [1m[38;5;153mglobalThis.location?.hostname ?? ""[0m — one character of defensiveness, module imports cleanly in Node,
    [38;5;153mresolveEnvironment[39m is directly testable, stays one file.
  - [1mSplit into two files[0m — a pure [38;5;153menvironments.js[39m and a browser-facing [38;5;153msettings.js[39m that wires in [38;5;153mwindow[39m. Cleaner
    separation, but an extra file in a project with six.

  I'd take the first. The optional chain is the whole cost, and splitting two dozen lines across two files buys nothing
  here.

  For the runner: [1mNode's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[0m ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m). It needs no dependency at all, which keeps [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m
  dependency-free — the thing you'd otherwise be giving up by adding tests. Vitest would be the alternative if you
  expect browser-environment tests later, but [38;5;153mresolveEnvironment[39m is a pure string function and doesn't need a DOM.

  Tests would cover: each known hostname maps to its environment, [38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m and [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m both resolve to local, unknown
  hostnames fall back to production, and [38;5;153mapiBaseUrl[39m is correct per environment.

